#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130406 :tutorial/content "Let's get a little more practice at writing regions in polar coordinates.\n\nConsider the semicircular disc of radius $1$ centered $(1,0)$ in the first quadrant, shown below. The equation of the curve is $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f4aec039a0de31d8f5ea24dc9db8c83f236dfd66e9f181768dae45ab0fd6b07.png)\n\nFirst, we recall that the following equations relate Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the function $y=\\sqrt{2x-x^2}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 2\\cos\\theta.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\cos\\theta$ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/35/3584d2f0d9ee2c0ce469bfc117c67c1efd1f4fd08c96c7beef00198624977d06.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/2c/2c5731478941621db03e8949eac217dcdfc604b3efd0312b56c59b9874d6be98.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos\\theta, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Writing More Complex Regions in Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130454 :tutorial/content "![](images/8f/8f2d689080add393fad398d45d992e595996a12c3eca60c1c97e9a3e14720ed1.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant (shown above) bounded by the lines\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 3, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3y-x}} = 6, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=4, \\qquad {\\color{red}{2x+y}}=6.\n$$\n\nThe area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$  \n\nLooking at the lines that bound our region, we consider the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu = {\\color{blue}{3y-x}}, \\qquad v = {\\color{red}{2x+y}}\n$$\n\nNote that this change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u ( x , y ) \\\\ v ( x , y )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3y-x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3y-x=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3 \\\\  & 3y-x=6 & ⟹\\, & u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v = 2x+y$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2x+y=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4 \\\\  & 2x+y=6 & ⟹\\, & v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 6, \\quad 4 \\leq v \\leq 6\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d55142f7713f3a9bfb93d1b1a383683af397a6edcd7c56a4338f25ab16b83a2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\nu = 3y-x, \\qquad v = 2x+y.\n$$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac17.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{7}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (6-3)\\cdot (6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130456 :tutorial/content "We can evaluate triple integrals by changing the variables to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the finite region enclosed between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a63e349d2776fe07077379b9d603e32ad2ff264cfa8005a226422b44f7e100fe.png)\n\n \n\nThis is a challenging integral to evaluate as it currently stands. However, we should recognize that the problem naturally lends itself to cylindrical polar coordinates.\n\nRecall that if $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b2/b2f8a6fe7f21b062eec294522e411c1e471b564eae68763d4767f917d65b54b0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $R$ is\n\n- bounded below by $z = 0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 1 - x^2 - y^2 = 1 - r^2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1-r^2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/da/dae76df462a695e655ffdde01559d19bf245aa6d601100fb8c7bc1024106a4c8.png)\n\n \n\nTo express our integral in terms of cylindrical polar coordinates, we will use the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, {\\color{blue}\\mathrm{d}V} = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nWhen expressing our integral in cylindrical polar coordinates, we must write \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}V}} = {\\color{blue}r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta}.\n$$\n\nIt's important not to forget the extra factor of ${\\color{blue}{r}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[white, 2pt]{{\\color{black}1}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[white, 2pt]{{\\color{black}1-{r}^{2}}}}\\bbox[white, 2pt]{{\\color{black}{r}^{3}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1}} \\int_{0}^{\\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black}1 - r^2 }} \\bbox[2pt,white]{\\color{black} r^3} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta\n=\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R x^2 + y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130457 :tutorial/content "Similar to transformations in the plane, we can also consider transformations of three-dimensional regions.\n\nFor example, the transformation\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} = \\mathbf{T}\\left( {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \\right) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\sin   v \\cos   w \\\\ u \\sin   v \\sin   w \\\\ u \\cos   v\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nmaps a rectangular prism in Cartesian $uvw$-space onto one-eighth of a sphere in Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e1ee5a6116d57cc1c3419b55bf0a9701b9f5d4d93931d053ed4a05b83a5d8f0.png)\n\n \n\nIn general, if a transformation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nis a $C^1$ transformation, meaning that it and its first derivatives are continuous, then the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor example, to compute the Jacobian determinant of our transformation above, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\begin{vmatrix}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\end{vmatrix}+u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot {u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡w+{sin}^{2}⁡w)+u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot u{sin}^{2}⁡v\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡w+{sin}^{2}⁡w) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's get some practice at computing Jacobians of three-dimensional transformations." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130460 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \n\nWe'll assume that $\\mathbf T$ is a $\\boldsymbol{C}^1$ **transformation**, which means that  $x$ and $y$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U$ contained in $\\Bbb R^2.$ \n\nThe **Jacobian determinant** of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}=\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe sometimes refer to the Jacobian determinant simply as the **Jacobian.**\n\nLet's compute the Jacobian determinant of the following transformation:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} = \\mathbf{T}\\left( {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\right) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\cos   v \\\\ u \\sin   v\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nTo compute the Jacobian determinant of this function, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-(-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =u{cos}^{2}⁡v+u{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll spend some time discussing the meaning behind the Jacobian shortly. But for now, let's get some practice at computing it." :tutorial/title "The Jacobian Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130461 :tutorial/content "To understand what the Jacobian describes, we recall our knowledge of affine transformations.\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the matrix equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = T\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf b,$ and $\\mathbf x$ are vectors from $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $T$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nFor an affine transformation, the determinant of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n- The *area scale factor* of the transformation:\n\nIf a rectangle in the $uv$-plane has area $A,$ its image in the $xy$-plane is a parallelogram of area $|\\det(T)|\\cdot A.$\n\n![](images/04/04f36d98bf7589d1faadeffee0812590337fd36d8bb44f5abe1920948dee5bb0.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *orientation-preserving* or *orientation-reversing:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) > 0,$ the transformation is orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/49/496a49ee541eab31f69f9c571e450547b0c6942b100bc8e24fbd927dd43e4d33.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) < 0,$ the transformation is orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/21/211369d6f3fc70bcf7cf40f3270c24c245d306247efc429cb85589e42c19ee07.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *invertible* or *non-invertible:*\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is invertible.\n\n![](images/e4/e4b840ad5a3efa082ac4056d1d9b7c03d2e3e11e016e053df055dbb2b39f0d1f.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{det}(T) = 0,$ the transformation is non-invertible. In such cases, a rectangle in the $uv$-plane collapses to either a line segment or point in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9a/9a038c5de0e9f3205194fd7941a4b371c695c51c294f2ad5ac0b8fc1015811d1.png)\n\n![](images/98/9813ba294a0242abbfb885ebd3e82505d05a2f8a1cfa3975c7859f9530cac8b1.png)\n\nAffine transformations are special because these properties are *global*. In other words, they apply *everywhere* in the plane. So, for example, if $\\textrm{det}(T) = 2$, then the image of *every* rectangle in the $uv$-plane is a parallelogram with double the area of its preimage." :tutorial/title "A Review of Affine Transformations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130462 :tutorial/content "The Jacobian determinant gives information about nonlinear transformations similar to that described by the determinant properties of affine transformations.\n\nThe critical difference is that, in general, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones. \n\nTo understand what this means, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}: U \\to \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a transformation, where\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ \n\nand we assume that $x(u,v)$ and $y(u,v)$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives on $U.$\n\nFor ease of notation, let's introduce $J(u_0,v_0)$ to denote the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0){:}$\n\n$$\nJ(u_0,v_0) = \\left.\\dfrac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nLet's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangle in the $uv$-plane whose bottom-left corner is the point $(u_0,v_0).$\n\n![](images/dc/dc084ec02cd0c4fac71379590e8dc4973354cacc898e267a0eb15a6e8500f31f.png)\n\nIt's important to note that the image of our rectangle in the $xy$-plane is typically *not* a parallelogram! However, provided that our rectangle is very small, its image can be *approximated* by a parallelogram (we'll justify this in a future lesson). \n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangle and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The *local* area scale factor of $\\mathbf T$. \n\nSuppose the length and width of our rectangle are $\\delta u$ and $\\delta v,$ so its area is $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v.$ Then, it can be shown that the parallelogram in the $xy$-plane has an area equal to $\\left|J(u_0,v_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta A.$\n\n![](images/be/be1771ebb041c418c58d7d5615510e2fcacfeaf3677fbace739498ef1998ef11.png)\n\nNote the similarity of this formula with that of affine transformations described earlier (we've simply replaced $\\det(T)$ with the Jacobian).\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)> 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving.\n\n![](images/8c/8c649fdd526a831fb990393b292fb4582dad14c2391d129e9ed59792ebb8ac31.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)<  0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\n![](images/ae/ae5a3d0a42e1c4b9f692994b89815382a30cdf3e676bbd9844258ad2a6961608.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)\\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\n![](images/33/33ca98206a347e13aa8ec179a52dd755008d9a7d74882391e0c3f2344f6f6641.png)\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\n![](images/3d/3d4534c263a9a5bb26ba35ce8f879cf01db741441d948c023acaa9407d58e6e0.png)\n\n![](images/65/659f26ac6aba808d66a760d5683f265fd7c54431d894b2eabdc255b46368fc24.png)\n\nPoints $(u_0,v_0)$ such that $J(u_0,v_0)=0$ are called **critical points.** Critical points are very important. We'll discuss these in more detail in future lessons.\n\nWe again stress that the Jacobian describes *local* properties, that is, local to the point $(u_0,v_0).$ The effect of a nonlinear transformation on an object varies as we move around the $uv$-plane, so its local properties also vary. So, for example, if a transformation is orientation-preserving at one point, it does *not* mean it's orientation-preserving everywhere!" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Jacobian"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130463 :tutorial/content "The **critical points** of a transformation $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^3\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^3,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{array} \\right]}  \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v , w ) \\\\ y ( u , v , w ) \\\\ z ( u , v , w )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nare the points in Cartesian $uvw$-space where the Jacobian is zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}=0\n$$\n\n  \n\nSimilar to the two-dimensional case, the transformation $\\mathbf T$ is locally non-invertible at the critical points.\n\nFinally, provided that $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible, the connection between the Jacobians of $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right)^{-1}\n$$" :tutorial/title "The Critical Points of a Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130464 :tutorial/content "Given a real interval $D = (a,b)\\subset \\mathbb R,$ the length of this interval can be represented by the integral\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int_a^b \\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhich we can also write as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nSuppose we define an invertible function $u = u(x).$ By the change of variables formula, the length of our interval can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Whenever we perform a change of variables, the domain of integration changes, too. This new integration domain is denoted $\\Delta.$  More technically, $\\Delta$ is the *image* of the $D$ under the action of $u(x).$\n\n- If we think of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}$ as a fraction, the $\\textrm d u$'s in our length formula appear to \"cancel out,\" leaving only the original $\\textrm d x.$ This makes the change of variables formula easy to remember.\n\nSo, to summarize,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(D) = \\int\\limits_D \\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nSince the length of our interval is simply $b-a,$ it's doubtful that we'll ever use this formula in practice! However, it does indicate how we might use a change of variables to find the *area* of a complex region in two-dimensional space. Let's explore this idea in more detail." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130465 :tutorial/content "![](images/6e/6ee5a19bf58db28574dc82aa3e6adcfd59015777bc503e66b9b9fd427278dcbf.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane shown above. The area of this region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nwhere we'll use the convention $\\textrm d A =  \\mathrm d x\\textrm d y$ when referring to integration with respect to the Cartesian coordinates $x$ and $y.$\n\nThis region looks pretty complicated. In such cases, it's often easier to find an invertible transformation $\\mathbf T$ (i.e., change of variables) whose inverse maps the region $D$ to simpler, preferably rectangular, region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane. \n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\n \n\nSo, rather than computing the required area by integrating with respect to $x$ and $y,$ we can instead perform the transformation and integrate with respect to $u$ and $v.$ \n\nTo carry out this process, we apply the **change of variables formula**\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$ \n\nNotice that this formula is analogous to the one we saw earlier for computing the length of an interval. In particular:\n\n- If we consider the Jacobian as a kind of fraction, the $\\partial(u,v)$ and $\\textrm d u\\textrm d v$ parts appear to \"cancel out,\" similar to the previous formula.\n\n- Note that $\\mathbf T$ could be orientation-reversing. We take the absolute value of the Jacobian to ensure that $D$ and $\\Delta$ have the same orientation.\n\nFinally, note that the change of variables formula assumes that $\\mathbf T$ is a $C^1$ transformation and has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$\n\nOften, the most challenging part is finding a suitable change of variables. The main idea is to get a nice rectangular shape with sides parallel to the axes in the $uv$-plane. In other words, we require a change of variables that reduces the equations of our curves to something like the following:\n\n$$\nu = \\textrm{const}_1, \\quad\nu = \\textrm{const}_2, \\quad\nv = \\textrm{const}_3, \\quad\nv = \\textrm{const}_4\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's look at a concrete example of computing an area using the change of variables formula." :tutorial/title "The Change of Variables Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130467 :tutorial/content "The Jacobian determinant of a spatial transformation can be interpreted similarly to the Jacobian of a plane transformation.\n\nAs before, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones. \n\nGiven that $J(u_0,v_0,w_0)$ denotes the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0, w_0),$ let's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangular prism in Cartesian $uvw$-space whose bottom, back left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0,w_0).$\n\n![](images/31/31c0a7275acdac0dc217e73f3c1e3d9efe504832419c87486cee980e20ea04da.png)\n\n \n\nIt can be shown that, provided that our prism is very small and $(u_0,v_0, w_0)$ is not a critical point of $\\mathbf T,$ the image of our prism can be approximated by a parallelepiped. \n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0,w_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangular prism and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The local *volume* scale factor of $\\mathbf T$. \n\nSuppose the length, width, and height of our rectangular prism are $\\delta u,$ $\\delta v,$ and $\\delta w,$ so its volume is \n\n$$\n\\delta V = \\delta u \\delta v \\delta w.\n$$\n\n Then, it can be shown that the approximate parallelepiped in $xyz$-space has a volume equal to \n\n$$\n\\left|J(u_0,v_0,w_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta V,\n$$\n\n as depicted below.\n\n![](images/d6/d669cd5369d33e618ab43cfd3e93ee70c9fea8420b5a829d78826c11524511aa.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0,w_0) > 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving. \n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps a triple of vectors onto a triple with the same orientation (right-handed $\\to$ right-handed or left-handed $\\to$ left-handed).\n\n![](images/f0/f05f9a79d9156518e948c46f6c67eb5ff31132fd4a088e14219eada7eb49a441.png)\n\n \n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0, w_0) < 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps a triple of vectors onto a triple with the opposite orientation (right-handed $\\to$ left-handed or left-handed $\\to$ right-handed).\n\n![](images/45/45b08e0fec7114db52a05cb42ed46d7d62baa5b01a5dea37752928348802b205.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0,w_0) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps our tiny rectangular prism (a three-dimensional region) onto a three-dimensional region (i.e., a parallelepiped).\n\n![](images/61/61557eb2d78d468946375b918ab182da518b7cf2c68a1c0a0593c93b991bcf78.png)\n\n \n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0,w_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps our tiny rectangular prism (a three-dimensional region) onto a two-dimensional region (a parallelogram), a one-dimensional region (a line segment), or a zero-dimensional region (a point).\n\n![](images/72/726ba518de982ddd0b2924a645463b496ed881ead76a652c812c7a164aa684b8.png)" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Jacobian"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130470 :tutorial/content "Let's now build some intuition behind the meaning of the volume element $\\textrm d V.$ This will help us to understand the change of variables formula in cylindrical polar coordinates. We start by considering the usual rectangular coordinate system.\n\nSuppose we have a point $P$ and a neighboring point $Q$ in the Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below. \n\n![](images/25/2535744585beb7fed89d760c0290a04f54d0a08c8f7e564b17c8e42432da031e.png)\n\n \n\nImagine moving from $P$ to $Q$ as follows:\n\n- First, we move a distance $\\textrm d x$ parallel to the $x$-axis so that the new point $P'$ has the same $x$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Then, we move a distance $\\textrm d y$ parallel to the $y$-axis so that the new point $P''$ has the same $y$-coordinate as $Q.$\n\n- Finally, we move a distance $\\textrm d z$ parallel to the $z$-axis to land to $Q.$\n\nThese movements span the rectangular solid shown above. The volume of this solid, denoted $\\textrm d V,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}V = \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y \\, \\mathrm{d}z.\n$$\n\nThus, the element $\\textrm d V$ represents the volume generated by moving from the point $P$ to the point $Q$ in this coordinate system." :tutorial/title "Deriving the Volume Element in Cartesian Coordinates"}]}
